WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.830
So I'm 20 years old, and like every
other girl, I love going to school.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.180
But I also like missing school one day or
two.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.550
I remember the first day I received
a notification from my teacher

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.570
saying that we wouldn't
have classes the next day.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.430
And then the next day I
received another notification

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.780
with one more day without classes.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.310
But then one day became one week, and

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:35.130
one week became one month, and
one month a couple of months.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:39.146
And then the entire school
year had to be readjusted for

00:00:39.146 --> 00:00:42.040
the amount of classes we were missing.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.108
Don't think I was on vacation
mode during that time, no.

00:00:46.108 --> 00:00:51.272
I would be walking down the streets
with a little pot my mom gave me and

00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:56.360
that I would use as a drum,
to go perfectly well with my chantings.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:02.041
[FOREIGN] Which means, not one step back.

00:01:02.041 --> 00:01:06.900
This was 2002 in Caracas,
Venezuela, in the first oil strike

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.950
opposition leaders organized to
protests against the government.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.900
This picture was taken a couple of weeks
ago, people still protesting in the same

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.350
street against the same government
17 years after that strike.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.770
If you happen to read the news,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.870
you've probably seen Venezuela in
the headlines over the past months.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:38.950
Words like hyperinflation, crisis, unrest,

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:45.230
are fairly common, but what I see in
this picture is larger than that.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:50.770
I see the hands of people I know, like
my mom and my dad that still live there.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.624
And more recently, I see hope,

00:01:54.624 --> 00:01:59.416
hope of some day being able to
come back to what I call home.

00:02:01.661 --> 00:02:06.463
With this talk I want you to understand
what is going on in Venezuela now, but

00:02:06.463 --> 00:02:10.380
I also want to show you
the nation behind the news.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:14.214
I want to you that despite the crisis,
there is a strong social

00:02:14.214 --> 00:02:17.993
fabric being built in Venezuela
that is worth standing for.

00:02:22.177 --> 00:02:25.110
But to do that let's travel
back in history for a bit.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:27.680
Recognize this face?

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.720
This is Hugo Chavez,
he was elected as president of Venezuela

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.530
in 1999 with a populous proposal
that came to change the traditional

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.200
politics of what was one of
the wealthiest countries in Latin America.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.400
He would stay in power for
14 years, until he died.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.720
During that time the price of an oil
barrel multiplied by ten, and

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.120
considering that we sit on top of
the largest oil reserves in the world,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.445
you would expect Venezuela to become
an even richer country, but no.

00:03:07.445 --> 00:03:10.859
Instead, the government
increase our foreign debt,

00:03:10.859 --> 00:03:15.534
destroyed our internal production
capacity, and fueled an overwhelming

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:19.040
amount of corruption that
left the country in mystery.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:26.880
By 2013, the year Chavez died, I was
graduating from the University, and I had

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.740
spent my five years in college fighting
against the government, all unsuccessful.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.279
New elections were scheduled and
Nicholas Maduro,

00:03:36.279 --> 00:03:41.438
announced by the government's propaganda
as Chavez's son, was elected president.

00:03:41.438 --> 00:03:44.460
He would stay in power for six more years.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.490
Terrified, I left the country

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.640
as part of one of the large
migration waves that fled Venezuela.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:04:00.550
Since then oil price plummeted, and years
of mismanagement, wrong public policies,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.650
and no investment in infrastructure
whatsoever, came to surface.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:12.170
Venezuela progressed towards one of the
worst socioeconomic crisis of our times.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:20.860
Please look at this graph, we are compared
to Syria, without actually being at war.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:28.905
Our annual inflation rate is estimated
in somewhere between 1.6 million and

00:04:28.905 --> 00:04:32.924
10 million percent, can you imagine that?

00:04:32.924 --> 00:04:37.420
90% of our population lives
under the poverty line,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.822
children have to scavenge for
food in the streets, and

00:04:41.822 --> 00:04:47.963
babies are dying in hospitals because
antibiotics are nowhere to be found.

00:04:52.539 --> 00:04:55.012
So what is happening now?

00:04:55.012 --> 00:04:57.333
By the beginning of this year,

00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:01.801
Maduro announced he would
continue with his presidency and

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:07.177
he has the support of a few countries,
like Russia, Cuba, and China.

00:05:07.177 --> 00:05:12.925
But this time, after years of
fighting against a government,

00:05:12.925 --> 00:05:16.074
we are fighting for a government.

00:05:16.074 --> 00:05:21.555
Juan Guaidó is the President
of the National Assembly.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.949
And he announced by the beginning of
this year that he would stepping in as

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:30.370
interim president.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.469
I have to explain a bit better
what all of these means.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:42.566
Years ago we elected
a National Assembly we all voted for,

00:05:42.566 --> 00:05:47.280
and Maduro last year

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.970
decided to strip away the power of this
National Assembly and created a new one.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.640
It is as if President Trump decided
to create a new Congress or

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.180
Republicans because the one
in power is fully Democrat.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.580
And even worse,
this National Assembly called for

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:09.550
presidential elections that were a fraud,
and that's where Maduro won.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:15.648
So Juan Guaidó, the President of
the National Assembly we had elected,

00:06:15.648 --> 00:06:18.896
is following articles
in our constitution and

00:06:18.896 --> 00:06:22.320
he's legitimately
announced his presidency.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:28.320
Immediately, millions of Venezuelans
started supporting him, and

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.770
that's the picture I showed
in the beginning of the talk.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.640
Not only Venezuelans, we have the support
of the international community.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.977
Over 60 countries have already
legitimized his presidency,

00:06:41.977 --> 00:06:48.915
including the US, the European Union,
and our neighbors, Colombia and Brazil.

00:06:48.915 --> 00:06:54.629
Now, don't get me wrong,
there's still a lot of uncertainties,

00:06:54.629 --> 00:06:58.250
and I don't really know what will happen.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.761
But maybe, just maybe, this time we do

00:07:02.761 --> 00:07:07.830
change things and a new government starts.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:16.140
For the first time in my life I felt
that coming back is a possibility.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:20.660
That the notion of rebuilding my
country is actually not that far.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.447
And that is an amazing feeling to have.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:31.936
I need you to understand something
about countries in crisis,

00:07:31.936 --> 00:07:34.180
there is never just one side to a story.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.308
That little girl that wandered the streets
in Caracas did not grow up in this

00:07:40.308 --> 00:07:40.976
reality.

00:07:40.976 --> 00:07:44.776
The country I love is much
more than these stats.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:50.503
This is where my family lives, this is
where I learned how to ride a bike,

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:53.120
this is where I did
the most mundane things,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.821
like watching The Notebook with my best
friend and crying silently over that.

00:07:57.821 --> 00:07:59.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:07.060
[LAUGH] And I can tell you something,
I am not alone.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:11.865
I can probably say that there a millions
of Venezuelan that love their country and

00:08:11.865 --> 00:08:15.160
that fighting now for a better future.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:20.530
Despite the daily struggles,
regular civilians, like you and

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:24.840
me, are solving for
the inefficiencies of a failed state.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:31.456
We are threading a social
fabric filled by creativity,

00:08:31.456 --> 00:08:35.430
innovation, and above all, resilience.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:41.587
As an example, if you hop in a bus in
Caracas today you might catch the bus TV,

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:47.554
this is an initiative created by a group
of young journalists that worried

00:08:47.554 --> 00:08:54.125
about the censorship of our national TV,
decided to ignite change themselves.

00:08:54.125 --> 00:08:57.431
And in a very simple, yet effective way,

00:08:57.431 --> 00:09:01.977
they showcase the news to
the lowest classes in Caracas.

00:09:01.977 --> 00:09:05.411
Like them,
we have medical students that created

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:09.520
first aid groups to support
in the violent protests.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.680
And we have hundreds of nonprofits that
are increasing their mission to cover for

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.480
basic needs.

00:09:15.612 --> 00:09:17.970
There's for example, the one I work with.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:23.120
That is originally focused in
sustainable tourism, but is now

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.080
reinvented itself to also provide energy
to communities that desperately need it.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.584
This is what we, Venezuelans,
are doing, but what can you do?

00:09:34.584 --> 00:09:39.880
Well, moving forward we'll require
a lot of help from abroad.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.720
And there are couple of ways
you could be part of it.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:46.880
First, through humanitarian aid and
donations.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.800
Second, through votes in favor of
certain decisions in your own countries.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.600
And three, by leaving this room and
sharing this message.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.439
Help me raise awareness.

00:09:57.439 --> 00:10:01.612
I hope that the next time that
you read new about Venezuela or

00:10:01.612 --> 00:10:07.290
any other country in crisis you're curious
enough to go beyond their surface and

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.490
look for the rebuilders.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:14.262
I can guarantee,
you'll be positively surprised, thank you.

00:10:14.262 --> 00:10:15.662
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:15.662 --> 00:10:20.715
[MUSIC]

